A||N||5||Motorhead||Electrical  "Shudder"   problem!||||1||
Z||000000||Motorhead||01-20-2001||08:20 PM||||Electrical problems always send me running away in frustration and cowering in the corner, but now I got one and need some help!<BR>Seems that when I apply the brakes I get an interuption in service to all the other utilities!<BR>The turn signals and brake lights seem to be affecting electrical service to other systems.  <BR>Symptoms include blinking of headlights and radio display when brakes are applied or turn signals given.  Sounds like a charging problem, but I get no idiot battery light when this occurs.  If it only occurred with the brake lights I'd say a faulty switch.  But it happens with turn signals also.<BR>Short in the taillights somewhere?<BR>I lose it when it comes to electrical disfunction, any help appreciated.<BR>OH YEA!  The battery went dead the other day for no particular reason.  Charged it back up, no problem since!<BR>Mike<BR><P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||01-20-2001||09:42 PM||||Sounds like the alternator is on it's way out. Does it also do it when you turn the fan on hi or the headlamps on? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||strdream||01-20-2001||09:47 PM||||Mike<BR>I was going to suggest you look for a wire that is rubbed bare by the brake peddle or arm but than you wrote that it happens whith the turn signal too. First off it sounds like a heavy short. It should be blowing fuses if it has enough load to bother the alternator.  I would check the fuses to see if some one has put a way too heavy fuse in that circuit. Next lets track it down. It could be in the rear wireing as the front lights are not used with brake lights.  Does it happen with Right and Left turn signal?  If it happens with both left and right than the problem is near the steering colunm between the brake light switch and the turn signal switch. If it is with one side only, than it is in the rear wiring.  I would look for a wire that is pinched in somthing or was a screw run into it or somthing like that.<BR>Ross    ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||01-20-2001||10:11 PM||||Check your belt and make sure it's tight.<BR>May also be a bad ground in the rear harness.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-21-2001||12:14 PM||||To expand a bit on what indy said,<P>you can get the charging system checked free at many places. call around. <P>A goofy ground is a real posibility. The brake and turn signals are tied to the parking lamp circuit via the rear lamps. if a ground is bad then the 2 circuits will try to use each other to reach ground, usually with wierd results.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Motorhead||01-22-2001||05:01 PM||||Thanks for the tips guys.  I'm home tomorrow so I'm gonna' pull out the multimeter and start tracking it down.<BR>New wrinkle.  Today I had a circuit go out completely for a while.  Speedo, radio, and temp gauge died and the brake warning light came on.  Everything started working normally again by itself after a few minutes.  <BR>I think the car is haunted!<BR>Mike<P>------------------<BR>"The one who dies with the most toys wins!"<BR>  bluewater46@hotmail.com<BR>1986 coupe<BR>1984 coupe<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1372350&a=10234998&p=38686771&Sequence=0"> ||||reg||1
